hat any language model can fully understand and follow the instructions without ambiguity. If the technical description refers to re\u2011implementing an existing software, do not include or mention the original software\u2019s name in the prompt. Do not add any role\u2011playing, personalization, or polite filler phrases (e.g., \u2018You are a software developer\u2019, \u2018As an expert\u2019, \u2018Please kindly\u2019). The prompt must remain strictly technical, objective, and instruction\u2011focused. Absolutely no simplified, mock, placeholder, dummy, simulated, or fake content is allowed. You must require the full software with (all) file(s), in complete, unabridged, production\u2011ready code. Read it letter by letter, line by line, from beginning to end\u2014you need to understand and remember every little detail! Always read and retain every single character of the provided text content in memory, ensuring no detail is overlooked.","answer":"Build a single, production-ready **prompt-writing system** that takes the provided technical description and rewrites it into one comprehensive, unambiguous prompt for a language model. The system must instruct the model to construct the entire target software from scratch, end to end, in complete unabridged code, while preserving the technical constraints and excluding any role-play, filler, placeholders, simulated content, or references to the original software’s name. \n\n## Core behavior\n\nThe prompt-writing system must first read and absorb the full provided technical description in order, from beginning to end, and then synthesize it into a single coherent instruction set. It must explicitly require that the resulting prompt describe exactly what will be built, how it will be built, and in what sequence the implementation should proceed, so that another model can follow it without ambiguity. It must also preserve the requirement that the output be strictly technical, objective, and instruction-focused, with no polite preamble, no persona framing, and no mock or fake content. \n\n## Construction rules\n\nThe rewritten prompt must demand the full software deliverable, including all files needed for a complete implementation, and it must state that the code must be production-ready and unabridged. It must also preserve the instruction that, if the description refers to re-implementing an existing software, the original software name must not appear in the final prompt. The output should read like a build specification for a system being designed from zero, not like a summary of an existing product or a vague creative brief. \n\n## Content handling\n\nThe system should convert the source description into one continuous prompt that covers setup, architecture, implementation steps, component behavior, file structure, and final deliverables in a single pass. It should not break the result into multiple prompts, commentary, or explanation layers. It should keep the technical content intact while reorganizing it into a clearer, more explicit sequence that a model can execute directly. \n\n## Style constraints\n\nThe rewritten prompt must remain strictly technical, with objective wording and no role-play, personalization, or filler phrases. It must avoid simplified, placeholder, dummy, simulated, or fake language, and it must explicitly require real implementation artifacts rather than illustrative stand-ins. The wording should be direct and complete, with enough precision that the prompt can serve as a standalone specification for generating the full system. \n\n## Required outcome\n\nThe final result of this prompt-writing system is a single prompt that fully instructs a model to build the requested software from scratch, step by step, from initial structure to final production-ready files. It should be comprehensive enough that the implementation path is obvious, the scope is fully covered, and no part of the system is left implied or underspecified.","created_at":"2026-09-01T09:03:48.681347Z","label":null,"query_status":"COMPLETED","engine_mode":"reasoning"}]}
